Cylinder & Graph Surface Flux Setup
 
1. Cylinder (Radius a)
 
- Unit normal vector:

\hat{n} = \pm \frac{\langle x, y, 0 \rangle}{a}

- Surface area element:

dS = a \, d\theta \, dz

 
2. Graph Surface z = f(x, y)
 
- Normal vector element (not unit normal \hat{n}):

\vec{n} dS = \pm \langle -f_x, -f_y, 1 \rangle dxdy

- Note: \hat{n} dS \neq \vec{n} dS (distinguishes unit normal from vector element)
 
 
 
Setting Up Bounds for Surface Integrals
 
For integrals of the form \iint_S \dots dxdy, determine bounds by examining the shadow (projection) of surface S onto the xy-plane.
 
 
 
Geometric Interpretation of Flux
 
If \vec{F} is a velocity field, the flux through surface S represents the amount of matter passing through S per unit time.
 
- Geometric breakdown for flux volume:
- Base = \Delta S (surface element)
- Height = \vec{F} \cdot \hat{n} (normal component of the field)
- Volume = \Delta S \cdot (\vec{F} \cdot \hat{n})